From e57ce08fcb60d4c6361b515fac204b39fb55bcee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CipherScope <dev@cipherscope.io>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 05:30:11 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 16/23] wallet: refuse P2MR receiving addresses where BIP360 is
 not deployed

Where the P2MR deployment never activates, a 32 byte witness v2 program is an
unknown witness program. Consensus accepts any witness for it, so coins paid to
such an address can be taken by the first miner that notices; the existing
functional tests spend exactly that way before activation. Offering a P2MR
receiving address on mainnet, testnet3, testnet4 or the default signet
therefore hands out an address that cannot hold money.

CheckP2MRReceiveAllowed() refuses when DeploymentEnabled() is false for
DEPLOYMENT_P2MR, and guards the two entry points that produce addresses meant
for receiving: importdescriptors, after which the wallet would treat the
scripts as its own, and deriveaddresses, which is the documented way to get an
address for a tmr() descriptor.

Parsing stays available everywhere, so getdescriptorinfo still parses and
checksums a tmr() descriptor off-chain. Loading a wallet that already holds one
is untouched as well, so funds stay recoverable if a chain gains the deployment
later. Signet is the one chain whose activation depends on configuration, so
wallet_p2mr_signet.py exercises both sides of the gate there.
